{"id":"gallstones","cards":[{"id":"gallste5183ef8","l":2,"f":"Pain after a heavy meal in a woman with fatty-food episodes — the hormone responsible","b":"<b>cholecystokinin (CCK)</b>","c":"Overview & Risk Factors"},{"id":"gallst1ef73d2e","l":1,"f":"<b>Cholelithiasis (stones only)</b> — Definition?","b":"Stones in gallbladder, <b>no</b> inflammation implied","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst20bc7569","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute Cholecystitis</b> — Definition?","b":"Inflammation of gallbladder wall, usually 2° to stone obstruction","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallstc1cab065","l":1,"f":"<b>Cholelithiasis (stones only)</b> — Wall thickness (US)?","b":"Normal — very thin","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst95106d2b","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute Cholecystitis</b> — Wall thickness (US)?","b":"<b>&gt;3 mm = thickened</b>","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst1f6e9a76","l":1,"f":"<b>Cholelithiasis (stones only)</b> — Wall appearance?","b":"Normal","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallsta32c7b3d","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute Cholecystitis</b> — Wall appearance?","b":"Edema / swelling, hyperemic, friable","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst3ec09928","l":1,"f":"<b>Cholelithiasis (stones only)</b> — Pericholecystic fluid?","b":"Absent","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallstae67d3a2","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute Cholecystitis</b> — Pericholecystic fluid?","b":"Present (from inflammation)","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst4264fd15","l":1,"f":"<b>Biliary Colic</b> — Response to analgesia?","b":"Improves within ~4 h","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallstc0d5560a","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute Cholecystitis</b> — Response to analgesia?","b":"Pain persists despite analgesia","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst7422c80b","l":1,"f":"<b>Biliary Colic</b> — Temperature?","b":"Normal","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallsta66f61cd","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute Cholecystitis</b> — Temperature?","b":"Fever","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst21f10b5b","l":1,"f":"<b>Biliary Colic</b> — WBC?","b":"Normal","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst483444b4","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute Cholecystitis</b> — WBC?","b":"Raised","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst86ff7b03","l":1,"f":"<b>Biliary Colic</b> — GB wall on US?","b":"No thickening","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst9384192a","l":1,"f":"<b>Acute Cholecystitis</b> — GB wall on US?","b":"Thickened / inflamed","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallstde113852","l":2,"f":"Key sign of gallbladder inflammation → <b>Murphy's sign</b> ; most sensitive and specific imaging for acute cholecystitis (keyed)","b":"<b>ultrasound</b>","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallstae1bd889","l":2,"f":"RUQ colicky pain for 5 days + slight fever, not improving with fluids → <b>acute cholecystitis</b> ; epigastric/hypochondrial pain, mild icterus, fever, leukocytosis","b":"confirm with <b>ultrasound</b>","c":"Cholelithiasis vs Acute Cholecystitis"},{"id":"gallst64ba2ed7","l":1,"f":"<b>Old Approach</b> — Strategy?","b":"IV fluids + antibiotics, let inflammation settle, delayed cholecystectomy weeks later","c":"Acute Cholecystitis — Operative Timing"},{"id":"gallstda3c7228","l":1,"f":"<b>Current Approach (preferred)</b> — Strategy?","b":"<b>Early cholecystectomy</b> during the acute admission","c":"Acute Cholecystitis — Operative Timing"},{"id":"gallst660b1e88","l":1,"f":"<b>Old Approach</b> — Rationale?","b":"Assumed less inflammation = easier dissection","c":"Acute Cholecystitis — Operative Timing"},{"id":"gallst4e8dbdc2","l":1,"f":"<b>Current Approach (preferred)</b> — Rationale?","b":"RCT evidence: no increased complication/conversion rate","c":"Acute Cholecystitis — Operative Timing"},{"id":"gallst141d41e6","l":1,"f":"<b>Old Approach</b> — Outcome?","b":"Longer overall treatment period","c":"Acute Cholecystitis — Operative Timing"},{"id":"gallst30d543dd","l":1,"f":"<b>Current Approach (preferred)</b> — Outcome?","b":"Definitive treatment immediately, shorter course, more efficient","c":"Acute Cholecystitis — Operative Timing"},{"id":"gallst1bc9316d","l":2,"f":"Small-bowel obstruction with a rounded RIF opacity and <b>air in the biliary tree</b>","b":"<b>gallstone ileus</b> ; air in the biliary tree = <b>pneumobilia</b>","c":"How Gallstones Cause Complications"},{"id":"gallst5c71e585","l":2,"f":"Upper GI bleeding + jaundice with <b>blood from the ampulla</b> after a recent road traffic accident","b":"cause <b>trauma</b> (haemobilia)","c":"How Gallstones Cause Complications"},{"id":"gallstd7fa65f2","l":2,"f":"Fever, jaundice, RUQ pain, tachycardia, cholestatic LFTs → <b>acute cholangitis</b> ; Charcot's triad → <b>RUQ pain, fever and jaundice</b> ; the symptom in the list linked to the triad (jaundice)","b":"keyed <b>pruritus</b>","c":"Ascending Cholangitis"},{"id":"gallst9ee85ad6","l":1,"f":"<b>Stable enough for endoscopy</b> — Management?","b":"Urgent biliary decompression with <b>ERCP</b> — do not unnecessarily delay to the next day","c":"Ascending Cholangitis"},{"id":"gallst952eedd6","l":1,"f":"<b>Severe obstruction/infection</b> — Management?","b":"Avoid excessive manipulation / forceful cholangiography — injected contrast may push infected material proximally","c":"Ascending Cholangitis"},{"id":"gallst6ee66e19","l":1,"f":"<b>Complete clearance unsafe/impossible</b> — Management?","b":"Place a <b>biliary stent</b> to decompress first, address the stone later","c":"Ascending Cholangitis"},{"id":"gallst0f42bc08","l":2,"f":"Acute cholecystitis (pericholecystic fluid, Murphy's)","b":"<b>cholecystectomy during the index admission</b>","c":"Case: Obstructive Jaundice from a CBD Stone"},{"id":"gallst202c45f3","l":2,"f":"Mild gallstone pancreatitis → <b>cholecystectomy in the same admission</b> ; gallstone pancreatitis with a dilated CBD → <b>ERCP</b> ; initial management of gallstone pancreatitis","b":"<b>IV crystalloids</b>","c":"Case: Obstructive Jaundice from a CBD Stone"},{"id":"gallst32d434c8","l":2,"f":"Cholangitis with 6 days of vomiting, T 39.6, dilated ducts","b":"first <b>IV fluids</b> (then antibiotics and ERCP)","c":"Case: Obstructive Jaundice from a CBD Stone"},{"id":"gallstc5cdc349","l":2,"f":"<b>95-year-old, poor ASA</b>, acute cholecystitis","b":"<b>percutaneous cholecystostomy</b>","c":"Case: Obstructive Jaundice from a CBD Stone"},{"id":"gallst43f68449","l":1,"f":"<b>Bile leak</b> — Mechanism / Notes?","b":"Inadequately secured cystic duct, or a small/accessory duct from the gallbladder bed → bile collection (biloma) → infection","c":"Complications of Cholecystectomy"},{"id":"gallst7737f3b0","l":1,"f":"<b>Bleeding</b> — Mechanism / Notes?","b":"Especially from the gallbladder / liver bed","c":"Complications of Cholecystectomy"},{"id":"gallst85e93104","l":1,"f":"<b>Major bile duct injury (rare, very important)</b> — Mechanism / Notes?","b":"CBD injury or right hepatic duct injury — prevented by a good Critical View of Safety before division","c":"Complications of Cholecystectomy"},{"id":"gallstfd635982","l":1,"f":"<b>Gallstone Obstruction</b> — Onset?","b":"Acute / sudden","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallstf2d2afe9","l":1,"f":"<b>Malignant Obstruction</b> — Onset?","b":"Gradual, progressive over weeks","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallst3b14b588","l":1,"f":"<b>Gallstone Obstruction</b> — Pain?","b":"<b>Painful</b> jaundice","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallstc357baf3","l":1,"f":"<b>Malignant Obstruction</b> — Pain?","b":"<b>Painless</b> jaundice","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallstdec455cb","l":1,"f":"<b>Gallstone Obstruction</b> — Typical patient?","b":"Younger, often female, RUQ/abdominal pain","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallsta34d5cb2","l":1,"f":"<b>Malignant Obstruction</b> — Typical patient?","b":"Older — classically 60–70 years","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallst27a9698f","l":1,"f":"<b>Malignant Obstruction</b> — Associated features?","b":"Weight loss, loss of appetite","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallste8c1ddbe","l":1,"f":"<b>Gallstone Obstruction</b> — Common causes?","b":"Choledocholithiasis","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallstcba31d52","l":1,"f":"<b>Malignant Obstruction</b> — Common causes?","b":"Pancreatic head carcinoma, distal CBD malignancy","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallst04a8b38a","l":2,"f":"Most common cause of acute pancreatitis → <b>gallstones</b> ; obese woman with gallstones, continuous mid-abdominal pain, vomiting, epigastric tenderness","b":"<b>acute pancreatitis</b>","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallstae543aec","l":2,"f":"67-year-old, painless jaundice, palpable gallbladder → <b>carcinoma of the pancreatic head</b> ; Courvoisier","b":"<b>palpable gallbladder: jaundice unlikely from stones</b>","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallstd3e31fae","l":2,"f":"70-year-old, progressive painless jaundice, obstructive LFTs → keyed <b>ERCP</b> ; jaundice, itching, weight loss for 6 months → <b>abdominal CT</b> ; US showing dilated intra- and extrahepatic ducts","b":"investigation of choice keyed <b>CT abdomen</b>","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallstd833f3bd","l":2,"f":"Jaundice least adversely affects (keyed)","b":"<b>vision</b> (pruritus/skin is affected)","c":"Obstructive Jaundice: Stone vs Malignancy"},{"id":"gallstfcbf5df5","l":1,"f":"<b>Pancreatitis (most common)</b> — Notes?","b":"From repeated pancreatic duct cannulation/manipulation","c":"ERCP"},{"id":"gallst28ea9685","l":1,"f":"<b>Bleeding</b> — Notes?","b":"Particularly after sphincterotomy","c":"ERCP"},{"id":"gallstd2e1ea6e","l":1,"f":"<b>Perforation</b> — Notes?","b":"Sphincterotomy-related → usually retroperitoneal; scope-tip bowel injury → may be intraperitoneal","c":"ERCP"},{"id":"gallst723503df","l":1,"f":"<b>Cholangitis</b> — Notes?","b":"Bacteria introduced into an obstructed biliary system","c":"ERCP"},{"id":"gallst7e53198b","l":2,"f":"<b>Acalculous cholecystitis</b> — the false statement","b":"\"<b>CT is the investigation of choice</b>\" (US is first-line; it occurs in critically ill patients, 5–10% of cholecystitis, aggressive course)","c":"ERCP"},{"id":"gallst705d4cdb","l":2,"f":"Severe pain 6 h after ERCP","b":"<b>post-ERCP pancreatitis</b>","c":"ERCP"},{"id":"gallstd0da2297","l":1,"f":"<b>Balloon extraction</b> — How it works?","b":"Balloon passed beyond stone, inflated, withdrawn to sweep it out","c":"CBD Stone Extraction Techniques"},{"id":"gallst6b310fe4","l":1,"f":"<b>Balloon extraction</b> — Notes / Limitations?","b":"Standard first approach","c":"CBD Stone Extraction Techniques"},{"id":"gallst411471fc","l":1,"f":"<b>Basket / mechanical lithotripsy</b> — How it works?","b":"Stone captured in a basket","c":"CBD Stone Extraction Techniques"},{"id":"gallst2a5d063a","l":1,"f":"<b>Basket / mechanical lithotripsy</b> — Notes / Limitations?","b":"Large/hard stones may not pass safely through the sphincterotomy — avoid over- enlarging it (perforation risk); may need lithotripsy to crush the stone first","c":"CBD Stone Extraction Techniques"},{"id":"gallstcd65242d","l":1,"f":"<b>SpyGlass cholangioscopy</b> — How it works?","b":"Small \"baby scope\" passed directly into the biliary system for direct visualization","c":"CBD Stone Extraction Techniques"},{"id":"gallst8fea2f04","l":1,"f":"<b>SpyGlass cholangioscopy</b> — Notes / Limitations?","b":"Alternative for difficult/impacted stones; stone fragmented then extracted","c":"CBD Stone Extraction Techniques"},{"id":"gallstc85a72bf","l":2,"f":"Cholecystitis complicated by choledocholithiasis","b":"<b>ERCP + cholecystectomy</b>","c":"Core Lecture Flow"},{"id":"gallst47641353","l":2,"f":"RUQ pain for <b>8 days</b> with fever (late presentation)","b":"keyed <b>delayed cholecystectomy in 2–3 months</b> (beyond the early window)","c":"Mastery Checklist"},{"id":"gallst881c6474","l":2,"f":"Obstructive jaundice, no fever, distended gallbladder, <b>2-cm CBD</b> and intrahepatic dilatation (elderly) → <b>CT with contrast</b> (look for a malignant cause) ; painless jaundice with a markedly dilated duct and intrahepatic dilatation in a 45-year-old","b":"<b>MRCP</b> (define the anatomy — e.g. choledochal cyst)","c":"Mastery Checklist"}]}